Here are 10 practical Kubernetes situations I’ve either faced myself or seen asked in interviews.

𝟏. 𝐀𝐏𝐈 𝐒𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐞𝐫 𝐒𝐥𝐨𝐰𝐧𝐞𝐬𝐬
The control plane feels sluggish. Ever wondered why the entire cluster starts misbehaving when the API server slows down?
Think resource starvation, high request rates, or even a misbehaving admission webhook.

𝟐. 𝐏𝐕𝐂 𝐓𝐡𝐚𝐭 𝐖𝐨𝐧’𝐭 𝐁𝐢𝐧𝐝
A Pod is pending forever, waiting on storage. You check the PVC, but it’s just not attaching.
It’s usually a mismatch between StorageClass, access modes, or missing dynamic provisioning settings.

𝟑. 𝐇𝐏𝐀 𝐈𝐬𝐧’𝐭 𝐒𝐜𝐚𝐥𝐢𝐧𝐠
You set up autoscaling, but nothing happens under load.
Turns out, metrics-server isn’t working, or maybe the target CPU percentage isn’t appropriate.

𝟒. 𝐂𝐥𝐮𝐬𝐭𝐞𝐫 𝐌𝐮𝐥𝐭𝐢-𝐓𝐞𝐧𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐲
You’re sharing a cluster across teams and need boundaries.
Namespaces, RBAC, NetworkPolicies, and ResourceQuotas are your best friends.

𝟓. 𝐍𝐚𝐦𝐞𝐬𝐩𝐚𝐜𝐞 𝐑𝐞𝐬𝐨𝐮𝐫𝐜𝐞 𝐐𝐮𝐨𝐭𝐚 𝐄𝐱𝐡𝐚𝐮𝐬𝐭𝐞𝐝
New Pods are not getting scheduled, and the reason is a silent quota breach.
kubectl describe ns helps. Prevent it by monitoring resource usage regularly.

𝟔. 𝐏𝐨𝐝𝐬 𝐒𝐭𝐚𝐫𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐒𝐥𝐨𝐰𝐥𝐲
They stay in the "ContainerCreating" phase for ages.
Common causes? Huge images, slow init containers, or probe timeouts.

𝟕. 𝐃𝐍𝐒 𝐑𝐞𝐬𝐨𝐥𝐮𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐅𝐚𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐈𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐝𝐞 𝐏𝐨𝐝𝐬
Pods can’t ping external domains.
Is CoreDNS working? Is the network policy blocking access? This one is tricky until you see it once.

𝟖. 𝐑𝐨𝐥𝐥𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐎𝐮𝐭 𝐚 𝐒𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐜𝐞 𝐌𝐞𝐬𝐡
You introduce Istio or Linkerd, and suddenly things break.
Gradual rollout, sidecar injection testing, and observability setup help prevent surprises.

𝟗. 𝐑𝐮𝐧𝐧𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐒𝐭𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐟𝐮𝐥 𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐤𝐥𝐨𝐚𝐝𝐬
Databases don’t like being treated like stateless apps.
StatefulSets, headless services, and proper volume claims matter here.

𝟏𝟎. 𝐄𝐧𝐟𝐨𝐫𝐜𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐒𝐞𝐜𝐮𝐫𝐞 𝐈𝐦𝐚𝐠𝐞 𝐒𝐨𝐮𝐫𝐜𝐞𝐬
Your security team wants to block random Docker Hub pulls.
Admission controllers, OPA Gatekeeper, and trusted registries become essential.
